            [
                new("Metadata and attributes", 7, "The compiler records every type and member in metadata. Attributes are extra metadata attached to them. An attribute does nothing on its own: [Obsolete], [JsonPropertyName] and [Authorize] only change behaviour because a framework reads them with reflection. The attribute object is created when you ask for it, so its constructor should be cheap and free of side effects.", "[Obsolete(\"Use OrderServiceV2\")]\npublic sealed class OrderService { }\n\nvar attr = typeof(OrderService).GetCustomAttribute<ObsoleteAttribute>();\nConsole.WriteLine(attr?.Message);  // constructed here, not at load time"),
                new("Reflection and its cost", 7, "Reflection lets you inspect and invoke members at run time. It also bypasses compile-time checks and is slow: it binds by name, allocates an argument array, boxes values and stops the JIT inlining. Activator.CreateInstance and MethodInfo.Invoke are far slower than a direct call. On a hot path, cache a compiled delegate or use a source generator.", "var method = type.GetMethod(\"Handle\");\nmethod!.Invoke(handler, new object[] { message });   // slow, boxes\n\nvar handle = (Action<Message>)method.CreateDelegate(typeof(Action<Message>), handler);\nhandle(message);                                     // fast, cached delegate"),
                new("Trimming, AOT and source generators", 6, "The trimmer removes code that nothing references. Reflection written with strings, such as Type.GetType(\"App.Foo\") or GetProperty(\"Bar\"), is invisible to it, so those members can be removed. Annotate with [DynamicallyAccessedMembers] or [RequiresUnreferencedCode]. Native AOT has no JIT and no Reflection.Emit. Source generators such as [GeneratedRegex] and System.Text.Json source generation move the work to build time, which is faster and trim-safe.", "var type = Type.GetType(\"MyApp.Handlers.OrderHandler\");  // may be trimmed\n\n[DynamicallyAccessedMembers(DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.PublicConstructors)]\nstatic Type Resolve(string name) => Type.GetType(name)!;")
            ],
            "Metadata, attributes and reflection are current on .NET 10, and the runtime supports MetadataLoadContext, [DynamicallyAccessedMembers] and [RequiresUnreferencedCode]. Trimming and Native AOT make reflection warnings real: treat IL2026 and IL3050 as errors in new code. Source generators are the recommended alternative on hot paths, and the framework uses them heavily.",
            "Assembly.ReflectionOnlyLoad and reflection-only AppDomains were replaced by MetadataLoadContext. Assembly.LoadFrom semantics and the GAC are .NET Framework-era; modern .NET uses AssemblyLoadContext and NuGet. Reflection.Emit still works on the JIT runtime but not under Native AOT. Binary serialization, which relied on reflection over [Serializable], is removed.",
            "Read section 1 and answer the attribute and reflection-cost questions (about 8 minutes). Trimming and source generators are a second sitting.",
            "metadata and attributes, attribute construction, reflection cost, compiled delegates, trimming and DynamicallyAccessedMembers, Native AOT limits, source generators and MetadataLoadContext.",
            [
                new("reflection-attribute", "multiple-choice", "When does an attribute's constructor run?", "[Obsolete(\"Use OrderServiceV2\")]\npublic sealed class OrderService { }", ["When something calls GetCustomAttribute or GetCustomAttributes and the attribute is materialised", "When the assembly is loaded, for every attribute", "When the C# compiler runs"], "When something calls GetCustomAttribute or GetCustomAttributes and the attribute is materialised", "Attributes are metadata. The compiler records the constructor arguments, but the attribute object is only created when a caller asks for it through reflection. That is why attribute constructors must be cheap and side-effect free, and why an unused attribute changes nothing at run time.", "Ask what turns the stored metadata into an object.", "Angular decorators run when the class is evaluated by the module system; .NET attributes stay inert until read."),
                new("reflection-cost", "prediction", "This method is called for every request. What happens to performance?", "var method = type.GetMethod(\"Handle\");\nfor (var i = 0; i < 10_000; i++)\n  method!.Invoke(handler, new object[] { message });", ["Reflection Invoke is far slower than a direct call and boxes arguments; caching a compiled delegate closes most of the gap", "Reflection is faster because the JIT caches the method", "Both compile to the same IL"], "Reflection Invoke is far slower than a direct call and boxes arguments; caching a compiled delegate closes most of the gap", "MethodInfo.Invoke performs argument array allocation, boxing and binding checks on every call, and the JIT cannot inline it. Caching a delegate (for example via CreateDelegate or a compiled expression) keeps the dynamic flexibility but turns the call into a direct invocation. Cache the lookup itself too: GetMethod is not free.", "Count the allocations and the indirection in one Invoke call.", "A dynamic property access in JavaScript is cheaper because there is no metadata binding step, but both pay for the indirection."),
                new("reflection-trim", "bug", "An AI assistant generated this handler lookup. It works in Debug and fails after publishing with trimming. Why?", "var type = Type.GetType(\"MyApp.Handlers.OrderHandler\");\nvar handler = (IHandler)Activator.CreateInstance(type!)!;", ["The trimmer cannot see the string reference, so the type may be removed or renamed; annotate or register it explicitly", "Trimming only removes unused NuGet packages", "Type.GetType always works because the type is in the same assembly"], "The trimmer cannot see the string reference, so the type may be removed or renamed; annotate or register it explicitly", "Trimming follows static references. A type named only by a string is invisible, so it can be removed. Add [DynamicallyAccessedMembers(DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.PublicConstructors)] to guide the trimmer, keep a static reference, or replace the lookup with an explicit registration that the trimmer can see.", "Ask which references the trimmer can prove are used.", "Bundlers such as webpack have the same limitation with dynamic import paths."),
                new("reflection-why", "explanation", "Why are attributes inert by themselves, and why is reflection expensive? Describe a scenario where reflection is the right choice and one where a source generator or compiled delegate is better.", "", [], "", "An attribute is metadata: the compiler stores the arguments, and the object is only created when a caller reads it through reflection, so an unread attribute has no effect. Reflection is expensive because it works through run-time type information: it binds members by name, allocates argument arrays, boxes values, performs access checks and blocks inlining and devirtualisation. Reflection is the right choice for genuinely open-ended extension - plugin discovery, a serialiser that must handle types it cannot know at build time, or tooling that inspects assemblies with MetadataLoadContext. A source generator is better when the set of types is known at build time (JSON contracts, regexes, request delegates) because it moves the work to the compiler and stays trim-safe. A cached compiled delegate is better when you must dispatch dynamically but the hot path matters.", "Separate the metadata model, the cost model and the two alternative strategies.", "Angular decorators plus reflect-metadata are the closest analogue; the build-time generator equivalent is a compiler transform."),
            ]),
